content.theory:
Java's `for` loop packs initialization, condition, and update into one line:

```java
for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
    System.out.println(i);   // 0 1 2 3 4
}
```

- `int i = 0` — start value
- `i < 5` — keep going while true
- `i++` — add 1 each iteration

The `while` loop runs while a condition stays true:

```java
int count = 0;
while (count < 3) {
    System.out.println("Tick " + count);
    count++;
}
```

The **do-while** loop always runs at least once (condition checked after):

```java
int n = 10;
do {
    System.out.println(n);
    n++;
} while (n < 3);   // prints 10 once, then stops
```

Loop controls:

```java
for (int i = 0; i < 10; i++) {
    if (i == 2) continue;   // skip
    if (i == 5) break;      // stop entirely
    System.out.println(i);
}
```

Count **down** with `i--` and `>`:

```java
for (int i = 3; i > 0; i--) {
    System.out.println(i);   // 3 2 1
}
```

